TYPE 3 — TREE PROPERTIES
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

When to use:
→ Compute a value that depends on the entire subtree structure
→ Height, depth, diameter, balance, symmetry, count of nodes

Key pattern: compute what you need from both children,
then combine to answer at the current node.

EXAMPLE — Maximum Depth of Binary Tree
https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-depth-of-binary-tree

Depth at null = 0.
Depth at node = 1 + max(left depth, right depth).

int maxDepth(TreeNode* root) {
    if (!root) return 0;
    return 1 + max(maxDepth(root->left), maxDepth(root->right));
}
EXAMPLE — Balanced Binary Tree
https://leetcode.com/problems/balanced-binary-tree

A tree is balanced if every node's left and right heights differ by at most 1.
Compute height and check balance simultaneously.
Return -1 as a sentinel value meaning "already unbalanced".

int height(TreeNode* root) {
    if (!root) return 0;
    int l = height(root->left);
    int r = height(root->right);
    if (l == -1 || r == -1) return -1;          // already unbalanced
    if (abs(l - r) > 1)     return -1;          // unbalanced here
    return 1 + max(l, r);
}

bool isBalanced(TreeNode* root) { return height(root) != -1; }
EXAMPLE — Diameter of Binary Tree
https://leetcode.com/problems/diameter-of-binary-tree

Diameter at a node = height(left) + height(right).
But the widest diameter might not pass through the root.
Track global max while computing heights recursively.

int ans = 0;

int height(TreeNode* node) {
    if (!node) return 0;
    int l = height(node->left);
    int r = height(node->right);
    ans = max(ans, l + r);    // update diameter passing through this node
    return 1 + max(l, r);
}
EXAMPLE — Symmetric Tree
https://leetcode.com/problems/symmetric-tree

A tree is symmetric if its left and right subtrees are mirrors.
Two subtrees are mirrors if: their roots match AND
left->left mirrors right->right AND left->right mirrors right->left.

bool isMirror(TreeNode* l, TreeNode* r) {
    if (!l && !r) return true;
    if (!l || !r) return false;
    return l->val == r->val &&
           isMirror(l->left, r->right) &&
           isMirror(l->right, r->left);
}

bool isSymmetric(TreeNode* root) {
    return isMirror(root->left, root->right);
}
EXAMPLE — Count Good Nodes in Binary Tree
https://leetcode.com/problems/count-good-nodes-in-binary-tree

A node is good if no value greater than it exists on the path from root.
Pass the max value seen so far on the way down.

int goodNodes(TreeNode* root, int maxSoFar = INT_MIN) {
    if (!root) return 0;
    int good = (root->val >= maxSoFar) ? 1 : 0;
    int newMax = max(maxSoFar, root->val);
    return good + goodNodes(root->left, newMax) + goodNodes(root->right, newMax);
}
