TYPE 4 — PATH PROBLEMS
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

When to use:
→ Find a path with a specific sum
→ Find the path with maximum sum
→ Count paths equal to a target

Two sub-types:

ROOT-TO-LEAF paths: pass remaining sum downward.
When you reach a leaf, check if remaining == node->val.

ANY-NODE paths: can start and end anywhere.
Use postorder — compute from children, combine at current node.
Use a global variable to track the best answer across all nodes.

EXAMPLE — Path Sum
https://leetcode.com/problems/path-sum

Root-to-leaf. Subtract node value on the way down.
At a leaf, check if remaining == 0.

bool hasPathSum(TreeNode* root, int target) {
    if (!root) return false;
    if (!root->left && !root->right) return root->val == target;
    return hasPathSum(root->left,  target - root->val) ||
           hasPathSum(root->right, target - root->val);
}
EXAMPLE — Path Sum II (collect all paths)
https://leetcode.com/problems/path-sum-ii

Same idea, but collect the path. Add node, recurse, remove node.

void dfs(TreeNode* node, int rem, vector<int>& path,
         vector<vector<int>>& res) {
    if (!node) return;
    path.push_back(node->val);
    if (!node->left && !node->right && rem == node->val)
        res.push_back(path);
    dfs(node->left,  rem - node->val, path, res);
    dfs(node->right, rem - node->val, path, res);
    path.pop_back();
}
EXAMPLE — Binary Tree Maximum Path Sum (Hard)
https://leetcode.com/problems/binary-tree-maximum-path-sum

A path can start and end at any node. It does not need to pass through root.
At each node, the best path through it = node->val + best from left + best from right.
But you can only return one side (left or right) to the parent — not both,
because the parent needs a straight path, not a fork.

int ans = INT_MIN;

int maxGain(TreeNode* node) {
    if (!node) return 0;
    int l = max(0, maxGain(node->left));    // ignore negative gains
    int r = max(0, maxGain(node->right));
    ans = max(ans, node->val + l + r);      // path through this node
    return node->val + max(l, r);           // return only one side to parent
}
EXAMPLE — Path Sum III
https://leetcode.com/problems/path-sum-iii

Count paths (starting/ending anywhere) that sum to target.
Use prefix sum + hashmap (same idea as subarray sum equals k).
Pass current prefix sum down. Check if (prefixSum - target) exists in map.

int dfs(TreeNode* node, long long cur, long long target,
        unordered_map<long long, int>& mp) {
    if (!node) return 0;
    cur += node->val;
    int count = mp[cur - target];
    mp[cur]++;
    count += dfs(node->left, cur, target, mp);
    count += dfs(node->right, cur, target, mp);
    mp[cur]--;    // remove on the way back (path ends here)
    return count;
}
