TYPE 5 — BST (Binary Search Tree)
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

When to use:
→ The tree has the BST property: left < root < right
→ Exploit this to search, insert, or validate in O(height)

KEY PROPERTY: inorder traversal of a BST gives sorted order.

EXAMPLE — Validate Binary Search Tree
https://leetcode.com/problems/validate-binary-search-tree

Do NOT just check left->val < root->val.
All nodes in the left subtree must be < root.
Pass valid range [min, max] down. Every node must fall within it.

bool validate(TreeNode* node, long long lo, long long hi) {
    if (!node) return true;
    if (node->val <= lo || node->val >= hi) return false;
    return validate(node->left,  lo, node->val) &&
           validate(node->right, node->val, hi);
}

bool isValidBST(TreeNode* root) {
    return validate(root, LLONG_MIN, LLONG_MAX);
}
EXAMPLE — Kth Smallest Element in a BST
https://leetcode.com/problems/kth-smallest-element-in-a-bst

Inorder traversal gives sorted order. Return the kth value.

int kthSmallest(TreeNode* root, int k) {
    int count = 0, ans = 0;
    function<void(TreeNode*)> inorder = [&](TreeNode* node) {
        if (!node) return;
        inorder(node->left);
        if (++count == k) ans = node->val;
        inorder(node->right);
    };
    inorder(root);
    return ans;
}
EXAMPLE — Lowest Common Ancestor of a BST
https://leetcode.com/problems/lowest-common-ancestor-of-a-binary-search-tree

Use the BST property to navigate without checking both subtrees.
If both p and q are smaller than root → LCA is in left subtree.
If both are larger → LCA is in right subtree.
Otherwise root is the LCA (they split here).

TreeNode* lowestCommonAncestor(TreeNode* root, TreeNode* p, TreeNode* q) {
    if (p->val < root->val && q->val < root->val)
        return lowestCommonAncestor(root->left, p, q);
    if (p->val > root->val && q->val > root->val)
        return lowestCommonAncestor(root->right, p, q);
    return root;
}
EXAMPLE — Lowest Common Ancestor of a Binary Tree (not BST)
https://leetcode.com/problems/lowest-common-ancestor-of-a-binary-tree

Cannot use BST property. Use postorder instead.
If current node is p or q → return it.
If both children return non-null → current node is the LCA.
Otherwise return whichever child returned non-null.

TreeNode* lowestCommonAncestor(TreeNode* root, TreeNode* p, TreeNode* q) {
    if (!root || root == p || root == q) return root;
    TreeNode* left  = lowestCommonAncestor(root->left,  p, q);
    TreeNode* right = lowestCommonAncestor(root->right, p, q);
    if (left && right) return root;   // p and q are in different subtrees
    return left ? left : right;       // both in same subtree
}
EXAMPLE — Construct Binary Search Tree from Preorder Traversal
https://leetcode.com/problems/construct-binary-search-tree-from-preorder-traversal

Use valid range [min, max] like validation. First value in preorder is root.
All values in (min, root->val) go left. Values in (root->val, max) go right.

int idx = 0;

TreeNode* build(vector<int>& pre, int lo, int hi) {
    if (idx == pre.size() || pre[idx] < lo || pre[idx] > hi)
        return nullptr;
    TreeNode* node = new TreeNode(pre[idx++]);
    node->left  = build(pre, lo, node->val);
    node->right = build(pre, node->val, hi);
    return node;
}
