COMMON MISTAKES AND HOW TO AVOID THEM
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

MISTAKE 1 — Validating BST only at adjacent nodes
Checking left->val < root->val is NOT enough.
All nodes in the left subtree must be less than root.
Always use the [min, max] range approach.

MISTAKE 2 — Forgetting the global variable pattern for diameter/max path
The answer does not always pass through the root.
Any time the maximum is across the whole tree (not just the root),
use a global variable and update it inside the recursion.

MISTAKE 3 — Not handling null nodes
Every tree function needs if (!root) return .
What base value? Think about what null means:
null height = 0, null sum = 0, null node = nullptr.

MISTAKE 4 — Returning both sides of a path to the parent
In maximum path sum, a path through a node uses both left and right.
But when returning to the parent, you can only extend in one direction.
Return node->val + max(left, right), not node->val + left + right.

MISTAKE 5 — Using BFS when DFS is needed (or vice versa)
BFS → level-by-level, minimum depth, right side view.
DFS → property of the whole tree, path sums, BST operations.
Mixing them leads to correct-looking but wrong code.

